WEBVTT

00:00.599 --> 00:02.360
Look at these two pieces of rock.

00:02.360 --> 00:04.080
They are the same granite. One

00:04.080 --> 00:05.759
was broken open recently. The

00:05.759 --> 00:06.919
other has been sitting at Earth's

00:06.919 --> 00:08.679
surface for a long time. Nothing

00:08.679 --> 00:10.480
carried it anywhere. It changed

00:10.480 --> 00:12.400
right where it was. That change

00:12.400 --> 00:13.960
is called weathering. And by the

00:13.960 --> 00:15.440
end of this video, you should be

00:15.440 --> 00:16.720
able to look at a rock and say

00:16.720 --> 00:17.840
what kind of weathering has

00:17.840 --> 00:18.480
happened to it.

00:18.761 --> 00:20.921
Most rocks form deep underground,

00:20.921 --> 00:22.681
under high pressure, and with very

00:22.681 --> 00:24.441
little water or air. When they

00:24.441 --> 00:25.641
are finally exposed at the

00:25.641 --> 00:27.641
surface, everything is different.

00:27.641 --> 00:29.160
The pressure is low. There is

00:29.160 --> 00:32.200
water, oxygen, and carbon dioxide.

00:32.200 --> 00:34.120
The rock is no longer stable, so

00:34.120 --> 00:35.801
it starts to break down. This

00:35.801 --> 00:37.760
happens in two ways. Mechanical

00:37.760 --> 00:39.520
weathering breaks rock into smaller

00:39.520 --> 00:41.321
pieces, but the pieces are still

00:41.321 --> 00:43.321
the same rock. Chemical weathering

00:43.321 --> 00:45.080
changes the minerals into different

00:45.080 --> 00:46.760
minerals. Here is a quick test

00:46.760 --> 00:49.321
you can use on any example. Ask,

00:49.321 --> 00:51.121
is it still the same material,

00:51.121 --> 00:52.641
only smaller, then it is

00:52.641 --> 00:54.360
mechanical. Has it become a new

00:54.360 --> 00:56.161
material, then it is chemical.

00:56.482 --> 00:58.001
The first kind of mechanical

00:58.001 --> 00:59.561
weathering comes from pressure.

00:59.561 --> 01:01.162
Deep underground, a body of

01:01.162 --> 01:02.561
granite is squeezed by the weight

01:02.561 --> 01:03.961
of all the rock above it. When

01:03.961 --> 01:05.562
that rock is worn away and the

01:05.562 --> 01:07.282
granite reaches the surface, the

01:07.282 --> 01:08.801
squeeze is gone. The granite

01:08.801 --> 01:11.122
expands and its outer layers crack

01:11.122 --> 01:13.201
and peel away in curved sheets. A

01:13.201 --> 01:14.281
little like the layers of an

01:14.281 --> 01:15.922
onion. Geologists call this

01:15.922 --> 01:18.002
exfoliation. If you see a rounded

01:18.002 --> 01:19.521
granite hill with curved slabs

01:19.521 --> 01:21.042
coming off it, you are looking at

01:21.042 --> 01:21.962
exfoliation.

01:22.283 --> 01:24.563
The second kind is frost wedging.

01:24.563 --> 01:26.603
Water runs into a crack. When it

01:26.603 --> 01:28.643
freezes, it expands and it pushes

01:28.643 --> 01:30.243
the sides of the crack apart.

01:30.243 --> 01:31.642
When the ice melts, the water

01:31.642 --> 01:33.403
sinks deeper and the next freeze

01:33.403 --> 01:35.562
pushes again. After many cycles of

01:35.562 --> 01:37.243
freezing and thawing, a piece of

01:37.243 --> 01:39.123
rock breaks off. Frost wedging

01:39.123 --> 01:40.483
needs temperatures that cross the

01:40.483 --> 01:42.443
freezing point again and again.

01:42.443 --> 01:44.002
That is why it is powerful in

01:44.002 --> 01:45.763
cold mountain climates and why it

01:45.763 --> 01:47.923
is very rare in Chennai. It never

01:47.923 --> 01:49.242
gets cold enough here for water

01:49.242 --> 01:50.323
and a crack to freeze.

01:50.644 --> 01:52.004
Two more kinds of mechanical

01:52.004 --> 01:53.604
weathering matter much more on a

01:53.604 --> 01:56.364
hot salty green coast. The first

01:56.364 --> 01:57.804
is salt weathering. Sea water

01:57.804 --> 01:59.644
soaks into the pores of a rock.

01:59.644 --> 02:01.204
On a hot sunny day, the water

02:01.204 --> 02:03.244
evaporates and salt crystals grow

02:03.244 --> 02:04.764
inside the rock. The growing

02:04.764 --> 02:06.724
crystals push the grains apart.

02:06.724 --> 02:07.964
Over time, this can leave a

02:07.964 --> 02:09.324
pattern of small pits called

02:09.324 --> 02:10.763
honeycomb weathering, like the

02:10.763 --> 02:12.724
sandstone in this photo. Remember

02:12.724 --> 02:14.603
that salt weathering is mechanical.

02:14.603 --> 02:16.564
The salt breaks the rock. It does

02:16.564 --> 02:18.564
not turn it into something new.

02:18.564 --> 02:20.763
The second is roots. A root grows

02:20.763 --> 02:22.644
into a tiny crack, and as it

02:22.644 --> 02:24.043
thickens, it forces the crack

02:24.043 --> 02:24.524
wider.

02:25.085 --> 02:26.805
Now for chemical weathering, it

02:26.805 --> 02:28.645
starts with rain. As rain water

02:28.645 --> 02:29.884
falls through the air and soaks

02:29.884 --> 02:31.245
through the soil, it picks up

02:31.245 --> 02:33.285
carbon dioxide. Water and carbon

02:33.285 --> 02:35.365
dioxide together, make a weak acid

02:35.365 --> 02:37.525
called carbonic acid. It is far

02:37.525 --> 02:39.005
too weak to hurt you, but given

02:39.005 --> 02:40.324
enough time it reacts with the

02:40.324 --> 02:42.165
minerals and rock. There are three

02:42.165 --> 02:44.765
reactions to know. In hydrolysis,

02:44.765 --> 02:46.285
a mineral reacts with water and

02:46.285 --> 02:47.724
becomes a different mineral. In

02:47.724 --> 02:49.724
oxidation, iron in a mineral

02:49.724 --> 02:52.285
reacts with oxygen. In dissolution,

02:52.285 --> 02:54.005
a mineral dissolves completely and

02:54.005 --> 02:55.445
is carried way in the water.

02:55.805 --> 02:57.566
Here is hydrolysis in a real

02:57.566 --> 02:59.725
rock. Both photos show the same

02:59.725 --> 03:01.926
granite. On the left is a freshly

03:01.926 --> 03:03.766
broken surface. The felt spar

03:03.766 --> 03:05.926
crystals are hard and glassy. On

03:05.926 --> 03:07.566
the right, is a surface that has

03:07.566 --> 03:09.526
been weathering for a long time.

03:09.526 --> 03:11.045
The felt spar has reacted with

03:11.045 --> 03:12.725
water and weak acid and it is

03:12.725 --> 03:15.006
turned into a soft chalky clay.

03:15.006 --> 03:16.645
The rock is now weaker, so it

03:16.645 --> 03:18.485
also breaks apart more easily.

03:18.485 --> 03:20.446
Chemical weathering works fastest

03:20.446 --> 03:22.246
where it is warm and wet. That

03:22.246 --> 03:23.846
describes Chennai very well, so

03:23.846 --> 03:25.446
expect chemical weathering to be

03:25.446 --> 03:26.726
doing a lot of the work here.

03:26.727 --> 03:28.686
The other two reactions are easy

03:28.686 --> 03:30.567
to spot once you know the signs.

03:30.567 --> 03:32.767
Oxidation is rusting. Many dark

03:32.767 --> 03:34.887
minerals contain iron. When that

03:34.887 --> 03:36.847
iron meets oxygen and water, it

03:36.847 --> 03:38.927
forms iron oxide minerals which

03:38.927 --> 03:41.167
are red, orange or brown. So a

03:41.167 --> 03:43.127
rusty stain on a rock or a deep

03:43.127 --> 03:45.846
red soil is a sign of oxidation.

03:45.846 --> 03:47.567
Dissolution is what happens to the

03:47.567 --> 03:49.327
mineral calcite, which makes up

03:49.327 --> 03:51.207
limestone and marble. Calcite

03:51.207 --> 03:53.367
dissolves in weak acid. Nothing

03:53.367 --> 03:55.406
solid is left behind. That is why

03:55.406 --> 03:56.967
the carve details on an old

03:56.967 --> 03:59.126
limestone or marble statue, slowly

03:59.126 --> 04:00.567
become smooth and blurred.

04:00.888 --> 04:03.008
One last idea, and it is the one

04:03.008 --> 04:05.128
people mix up most. Weathering

04:05.128 --> 04:07.048
happens in place. The rock breaks

04:07.048 --> 04:08.447
down, but it does not go

04:08.447 --> 04:10.608
anywhere. Erosion is what happens

04:10.608 --> 04:13.808
next. Water, wind, ice or gravity,

04:13.808 --> 04:15.447
picks up the loose material and

04:15.447 --> 04:17.607
carries it away. Deposition is

04:17.607 --> 04:19.007
when that material is dropped

04:19.007 --> 04:20.687
somewhere new. The three work

04:20.687 --> 04:22.808
together. When erosion clears away

04:22.808 --> 04:24.728
the loose pieces, fresh rock is

04:24.728 --> 04:26.648
exposed and weathering can start

04:26.648 --> 04:28.048
on it. So when you look at a

04:28.048 --> 04:30.607
landscape, ask two questions. What

04:30.607 --> 04:32.167
broke this rock down, and what

04:32.167 --> 04:33.167
moved the pieces?

04:33.168 --> 04:35.368
Before you move on, check yourself

04:35.368 --> 04:37.087
with three questions. Pause the

04:37.087 --> 04:39.688
video if you need to. One. Salt

04:39.688 --> 04:41.208
crystals grow inside a rock on

04:41.208 --> 04:42.608
the coast and push the grains

04:42.608 --> 04:44.408
apart. Is that mechanical or

04:44.408 --> 04:46.368
chemical? Two. Felt spar and

04:46.368 --> 04:48.408
granite slowly turns into clay.

04:48.408 --> 04:50.808
Mechanical or chemical? Three.

04:50.808 --> 04:53.088
Monson rain washes red soil off a

04:53.088 --> 04:55.168
hillside and into a drain. Is

04:55.168 --> 04:57.568
that weathering or erosion? Here

04:57.568 --> 04:59.248
are the answers. The salt is

04:59.248 --> 05:00.968
mechanical because the rock is

05:00.968 --> 05:03.568
broken, not changed. The felt spar

05:03.568 --> 05:05.328
is chemical, because a new mineral

05:05.328 --> 05:07.448
forms, and the soil in the drain

05:07.448 --> 05:09.247
is erosion, because the material

05:09.247 --> 05:11.008
has moved. Now go on to today's

05:11.008 --> 05:11.448
task.
